      <div title="2:02 PM" class="update_at">Oct 11, 2026</div><div class="username">
          <a href="https://myanimelist.net/profile/Wiz-Pip" class="ga-click" data-ga-click-type="review-anime-reviewer">Wiz-Pip</a>
          </div><div class="tags"><div class="tag mixed-feelings btn-label js-btn-label" data-id="2" data-href="https://myanimelist.net/anime/62936/Toumei_na_Yoru_ni_Kakeru_Kimi_to_Me_ni_Mienai_Koi_wo_Shita/reviews"><i class="fas fa-star-half-stroke"></i>Mixed Feelings</div></div>      <div class="text">
                  <div class="thumb-right">
            <a href="https://myanimelist.net/anime/62936/Toumei_na_Yoru_ni_Kakeru_Kimi_to_Me_ni_Mienai_Koi_wo_Shita" class="ga-click" data-ga-click-type="review-anime-title-pic">
              <img width="48" data-src="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1145/158339.jpg?s=4a9000920d371ba604c2cf4cb79e0c4b" data-srcset="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1145/158339.jpg?s=4a9000920d371ba604c2cf4cb79e0c4b 1x, https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/96x142/images/anime/1145/158339.jpg?s=3bdee4395488197f977b9ee9a6c26546 2x" class="lazyload" />
            </a>
          </div>
                        I would consider myself a bit of a safe person in terms of anime taste; I tend to like what other people like, and I tend to dislike widely disregarded content. It’s extremely rare that I look at a score on this website and strongly disagree, except for comedies (which is so subjective that I hardly count it). <br />
<br />
This, this right here is the most dumbfounded I’ve ever been with a MAL score. Love Unseen Beneath the Clear Night Sky (or KakeKoi apparently) outscores Jaadugar: A Witch in Mongolia, the first season of You and I are
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">Polar Opposites, Fate/Strange Fake, Goodbye Lara, and is close to the score of Witch Hat Atelier and Nippon Sangoku (I haven’t seen this one, but people seem to love it). I don’t like to focus on scores, I don’t think it’s productive, but I’m just trying to illustrate my expectations going into this as supposedly one of the greatest anime to come out this year.<br />
<br />
	This most certainly is not one of the greatest this year; not even close.<br />
<br />
	KakeKoi is really a tale of two halves. The first half is a romance between Koharu Fuyutsuki and Kakeru Sorano, with the “gimmick” of this romance being that our female lead happens to be blind. This worried me at first, as I initially feared Koharu’s blindness would simply be used as a means to empower the male protagonist. Luckily this is not the case. I am not a physically disabled person; however, the show seems to handle Koharu’s blindness with great care. There are themes sprinkled in about self-acceptance and a desire to be engaged with without her blindness being a centerpiece for attention. Unfortunately, outside of Koharu’s blindness the characters themselves are extremely uninteresting. While Kakekoi exceeds expectations with writing Koharu’s blindness, she doesn’t have anything else to offer as a character besides her blindness and her relationship with Kakeru. The viewer knows nothing about Koharu besides those two things. Kakeru’s even worse; the viewer is offered another light novel romance with a generic self-insert protagonist. Kakeru also has only two distinct traits: his relationship with Koharu and the fact that his parents are divorced. Unlike Koharu, his divorced parents serve zero purpose outside of justifying his antisocial behavior. This isn’t necessarily a bad thing, there was just no real attention given to this fact outside of being told about it. It’s made worse that another light novel romance JUST came out last season that executed the divorced parents derived antisocial behavior better. <br />
<br />
	I don’t think the first half is awful, mind you; there’s great ideas sprinkled in there. I’ve already talked about how Koharu’s blindness is handled, but dialogue in the show outside of the two leads is pretty decent. There are a few scenes in the show where several characters discuss where each of them grew up which I appreciated. The fact that this takes place in college sets this show apart from its contemporaries in general. The first half has its issues but it’s by no means bad, even if I was personally bored.<br />
<br />
	It turns out that the show got bored of itself and instead attempted to be Your Lie in April. The 7th episode hits the ground running with a shocking reveal regarding Koharu, and the story quickly spirals away from a romance and instead morphing into a tragic drama. The entire thing feels extremely forced; gone are the interesting themes discussing Koharu’s blindness, gone is the advantage of being in a college setting. The first half of the show tells you to engage with Koharu as a person and not endlessly pity her but then turns around and demands you pity her in this second half. It’s unbelievable how forced all of this feels. It’s not like this came out of nowhere, there’s some dialogue in the early episodes that suggested something was up with Koharu, but the two halves of this show could not work worse together. The show wants you to cry so badly but it doesn’t have the character writing to demand your sympathy, and it most certainly does not have the cinematography or soundtrack to compliment the scenes asking you to cry.<br />
<br />
	Even with how jarring and forced this all feels, I still had doubts within myself. Maybe I’m being overly critical to this second half because I was unengaged during the first half. Absolutely nothing could have possibly prepared me for the finale of this show. I take everything back, none of this was forced, this was all an elaborate setup for the corniest final 5 minutes I have seen in a long time. American Idol wishes it could set up a more blatant sob story. I can’t even tell you any more about how stupid this ending is without spoiling the show, but what I can tell you is that the suffering Olympics baton gets handed to KAKERU, because we need to cry for HIM too! This is ALL in the last 5 minutes of the show with no prior buildup and serves exclusively to remind our protagonist to never give up, because Koharu never gave up too. <br />
<br />
	KakeKoi isn’t lazy with its writing, nor is it insincere. There’re genuinely interesting ideas that this show offers that set it apart. Unfortunately, more than anything, it is corny. It is a genuine attempt at a heartfelt story that spurs a suffering Olympics that make the closure of this series to be pitifully unserious and laughably corny. <br />
<br />
5/10 – Thank you for reading.</span>
              </div>
