                -🟢Spring 2026<br />
Witch Hats, Wasted Potential xD<br />
<br />
A 13-episode anime that ends before anything interesting actually happens. It serves as an introduction to a creative world that nonetheless feels lifeless. This is arguably the least engaging first season of a potentially interesting series, as it ignores everything that makes the premise compelling..........<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
Plot:<br />
&quot;Coco dreams of becoming a sorceress, although magic is only available to the initiated. When she discovers the secret of its operation, she unknowingly brings about a tragedy. Under the tutelage of the wizard Qifrey, she begins to learn magic, learning its possibilities, limitations, and dangers.&quot;<br />
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;"><br />
<br />
--If I had to summarize this anime with a comparison, I would say it&#039;s &quot;Harry Potter + Star Wars as medieval fantasy,&quot; but it pushes the most interesting elements aside and focuses mainly on simple and predictable didactic plots. After all, it&#039;s also an anime about school and the learning of magic by a group of little girls.<br />
<br />
 We have two groups: the Pointy Hats (Jedi) and the Brimmed Hats (Sith). The former help ordinary people and keep the secrets of magic secret to prevent wars that broke out during the era of magic&#039;s prevalence. The latter are extremists who (it&#039;s not certain, as their storyline has only been touched on briefly this season) want to remove all restrictions on magic so that everyone can use it to fulfill their ambitions and dreams and push the boundaries of what&#039;s possible.<br />
This seems interesting. The witch community wants magic to serve people, but in the name of the greater good, they impose restrictions on it (spells can only be placed on objects) that not only prevent the abuse of magic and wars but also deny access to magic that could heal and change the lives of many people for the better.<br />
Healing magic is the only puzzling aspect of witchcraft law presented to us. On the one hand, influencing someone&#039;s mind is permitted for the greater good to maintain the system, but healing magic is absolutely forbidden, and aside from mentioning this, the anime doesn&#039;t elaborate on the issue.<br />
<br />
 And that&#039;s exactly my complaint; all these interesting things were barely mentioned in these 13 episodes.<br />
This anime leaves the most interesting storylines for later and instead stuffs us with child protagonists&#039; storylines that reek of shounen-like pursuits of growth and becoming stronger, but with a more didactic emphasis.<br />
The comparison to Star Wars is not without reason; a much more interesting plot direction would have been to balance the characters&#039; temptations to turn to the dark side... that is, to use forbidden magic, yet this anime completely ignores this throughout these 13 episodes.<br />
<br />
The most interesting developments regarding the main cast revolve around the white-haired teacher, who is divided into two equally authentic personalities:<br />
- A kind-hearted teacher who has a very good attitude towards his students and their education.<br />
- A ruthless maniac who will do anything to find the sinister witch organization.  However, these two personalities never clash. Qifrey is a good-natured teacher most of the time, but the things he does to find the brimmed hats never constitute a contradiction that would trigger any internal conflict within him. On the one hand, he cares about the girls, BUT he never considers that his search might lead to them being harmed.<br />
<br />
Clearly, the most interesting character seems inconsistently written, which makes him more irritating than intriguing.<br />
His character is also linked to the fact that despite facing dangerous extremists, no precautions are ever taken, and no one ever focuses on safety.<br />
Qifrey and Coco, despite being aware of the threat, never even address it. Everything unfolds as if Coco had just arrived at the magic school and there was no threat. Despite numerous dangerous situations caused by the antagonists, nothing ever changes.<br />
Worse still, the plot is often pushed forward by the sheer stupidity of the characters, especially Qifrey, who, after a significant incident with the antagonists, once again leaves Coco alone, not to mention previously leaving his students without any communication, despite the fact that he keeps a potentially problematic girl at his school at a high price for his own purposes... the credibility of the script is seriously damaged.<br />
<br />
 The rest of the characters, for the most part, aren&#039;t bad (not you, Tetia). Almost all of them are different and have different approaches to magic and science, but their storylines seem uninteresting compared to the truly interesting things this anime could have explored. Perhaps they would have had a better chance if it weren&#039;t for the fact that the main cast is mostly children, so we get a lot of typical school storylines, including things like uncertainty about one&#039;s abilities even at the beginning and the need to understand that it takes time, the struggles of meeting family expectations, and the general theme of adults forcing children to follow a single path, ignoring their individuality.<br />
<br />
These storylines are also hampered by excessive exposition, where characters explain everything thoroughly. &quot;Show, don&#039;t tell&quot; is a rarity here.<br />
More than once, I&#039;ve found myself scratching my head as a character explains something they already know in an internal monologue.<br />
<br />
 The main thread running through the entire anime is Coco and Agott&#039;s relationship, which is written well for the most part, but only serves to develop Agott&#039;s character. The relationship from Coco&#039;s perspective seems truly shallow and bland, as her character seems unwavering, and the anime only gives the illusion that she has some significant internal conflict.<br />
She will never give up and will keep trying until Agott understands that Coco is worthy of being a witch... that&#039;s it, nothing interesting. No matter how many times Agott rejects her, Coco will always try to establish a relationship with her.<br />
It&#039;s simply bland, predictable, and boring, yet it constitutes a significant portion of the content of these 13 episodes.<br />
<br />
Therefore, the biggest problem with this anime is its main character. Coco, as the main/most important character, is simply hopeless.<br />
As I said, this anime only pretends that this character has any real internal conflict and well-developed motivations.  Her character is simply a good-natured girl obsessed with magic, which doesn&#039;t stray beyond the typical boring shounen protagonist who infects everyone with her optimism and inspires admiration for her pure intentions.<br />
Any struggles she has are small things that disappear as quickly as they appear.<br />
Her character doesn&#039;t pursue any real path or struggle with anything beyond trying to become better at magic.<br />
<br />
How does this anime begin? With a serious tragedy that kicks off the entire plot—yet the show ignores all the consequences of that event.<br />
For a protagonist who has endured such trauma, a natural storyline would involve grappling with guilt and the fear of the very thing that caused the tragedy.<br />
Instead, these elements are merely touched upon briefly rather than developed into genuine plot threads.<br />
Despite losing her mother, Coco never truly confronts her grief or attempts to come to terms with the situation; she is simply driven, never reflecting deeply on anything. The subplot regarding her mother is so insignificant that it feels as though the tragedy never happened—as if Coco had simply left home to study. Her mother’s story serves merely as a source of motivation, yet the narrative foundation for it is abysmal—from Coco’s internal experience to the lack of any established relationship between her and her mother.<br />
This dramatic element is handled so carelessly that it fails to make any impact and feels completely forced.<br />
<br />
Coco’s character is equally shallow, even when it comes to the magic itself.  Her love for magic is bland and shallow—a mere fascination sparked by the pretty lights she saw in childhood (the beauty of the magic lies mostly in the lovely animation; kudos to the studio). At no point does it evolve into anything more than &quot;Oh, I’m a good girl, so I’ll use magic to help people.&quot; Even traumatic events involving forbidden magic fail to change this perspective or prompt any deeper questions; everything is resolved with the simple notion that &quot;forbidden magic is bad, *but* the kind that isn&#039;t forbidden is cool&quot;—and that’s it. Similarly, she never spends much time wondering what those wide-brimmed hats want from her, nor does she worry about the &quot;morality police&quot; threatening to strip away her memories... Why? Because she isn&#039;t a believable character; she’s just a puppet in the author&#039;s hands—a puppet placed at the center of events and given the most screen time in the anime... unfortunately.<br />
<br />
World-building is the most interesting aspect here; however, rather than praising the creativity of the magic system, I’ll focus on the flaws—specifically, the world&#039;s sense of emptiness. After all, even the best magic system can&#039;t prevent a lifeless world from breaking your immersion.<br />
<br />
Let’s start with the fact that the way the world is presented doesn&#039;t match the nature of the world itself.<br />
There is no sense of the world&#039;s vastness conveyed through travel. Of course, not every fantasy story requires a journey—take *Harry Potter*, for instance: it features a massive, mysterious school and a few interesting locations nearby, which the characters explore without needing to go on a long trek.  WHA features a vast world, yet it doesn&#039;t feel that way; there are only a few interesting locations the characters teleport to, while the rest consists of the heroes&#039; atelier and that single city they constantly visit.<br />
A solution to this could have been a multitude of storylines—like in *Game of Thrones*—showing various regions of the world, rather than keeping us confined to one place with only occasional teleports to more interesting maps that nonetheless feel enclosed, causing the world to lose its sense of scale.<br />
<br />
I also have a problem with the society in this anime.<br />
<br />
Starting with the witch authorities, who guard the secret of magic, but we never see any action taken when it&#039;s common knowledge that someone outside the witch world has discovered the secret of Maggi. Okay, it&#039;s said that Qifey was given permission, but that also kills any credibility because in the witch world, where keeping the secret of Maggi is paramount, absolutely no one reacts to someone who &quot;accidentally&quot; discovers magic, and no one tries to investigate, no one asks questions, there are no factions, someone allows something, and everyone agrees?<br />
<br />
The truly lazy worldbuilding makes the first half of this anime (the second half, actually, too, but I don&#039;t feel like writing about it) completely devoid of tension, even though the premise of a girl who could be a &quot;threat to the system&quot; requires that the system constantly put pressure on her... but as I said before, Coco&#039;s life is no different than if she had simply gone to a school with internet access to learn.<br />
<br />
 The ban on magic for most people and the erasure of their memories upon learning its secrets is quite oppressive, and it&#039;s an interesting theoretical element of this world, but the viewer never really feels this, not least because of how Coco&#039;s case is treated, but also because, apart from Coco&#039;s, we don&#039;t hear about any other cases of people outside of witch society learning the secrets of magic.<br />
People don&#039;t mention any other similar cases when they learn of Coco&#039;s existence, the moral police are never shown in any context other than the events of Coco. Nothing actually happens in this world, nothing is foreshadowed, no one (aside from a small, mysterious group of extremists who hide) is shown to be actually, even politically, acting on the issue of access to magic, no conflicts between witches and ordinary people... a void that makes the &quot;magic is a secret&quot; premise lose its real meaning, and the world seems to cease to exist beyond our characters.<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
 Someone who&#039;s read the manga might say, &quot;Oh, but it gets interesting later.&quot; Okay, I&#039;ll rate it higher later, but these 13 episodes were completely unsatisfactory. The anime focused on generic, often poorly written storylines, and barely touched on the most interesting plotlines, leaving them &quot;for later.&quot;<br />
<br />
Rating: ⭐⭐⭐⭐❌❌❌❌❌❌</span>
