    <div class="body">
      <div title="12:10 PM" class="update_at">Oct 11, 2026</div><div class="username">
          <a href="https://myanimelist.net/profile/FinnC200" class="ga-click" data-ga-click-type="review-anime-reviewer">FinnC200</a>
          </div><div class="tags"><div class="tag mixed-feelings btn-label js-btn-label" data-id="2" data-href="https://myanimelist.net/anime/50425/Fuufu_Ijou_Koibito_Miman/reviews"><i class="fas fa-star-half-stroke"></i>Mixed Feelings</div></div>      <div class="text">
                  <div class="thumb-right">
            <a href="https://myanimelist.net/anime/50425/Fuufu_Ijou_Koibito_Miman" class="ga-click" data-ga-click-type="review-anime-title-pic">
              <img width="48" data-src="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1713/126442.jpg?s=863dc2e541f7a0c1ab824bbc33aff7cf" data-srcset="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1713/126442.jpg?s=863dc2e541f7a0c1ab824bbc33aff7cf 1x, https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/96x142/images/anime/1713/126442.jpg?s=5d0e8ae7dd590352c921de79d397c83a 2x" class="lazyload" />
            </a>
          </div>
                I had a day off after working non-stop for a few days and just wanted to disconnect, so I binged this entire anime in one sitting.<br />
<br />
And honestly, as pure mindless entertainment, it works pretty well. That said, we’re not going to pretend the clichés aren’t extremely obvious.<br />
<br />
The first thing that bothered me from episode one was the whole “academic practical” premise. These are minors who are forced to fake a relationship with a randomly assigned classmate, even if they’re already in love with someone else and feel absolutely nothing for their assigned partner.<br />
<br />
Are you really going to force a relationship that one of them doesn’t
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">feel just to pass some school assignment? Imagine being in love with someone (like the protagonist is) and having to live as a couple with a person you don’t even like. Wouldn’t it make more sense to be able to explain the situation and, if you genuinely feel uncomfortable, switch to a different project?<br />
<br />
There are episodes I genuinely enjoyed, like the fireworks one, but the series also leans pretty hard into fanservice. The girls all look like magazine models, every single one of them is unrealistically attractive, while most of the guys look completely average, just like Jirou.<br />
<br />
Then there’s the romantic fantasy aspect. Yes, the show entertains me and lets me turn my brain off, which is exactly what I was looking for. But it also feels like a pretty unattainable fantasy for people who are single and end up projecting themselves into the story, imagining they could be the protagonist.<br />
<br />
And here’s the funny part: I’m single too, but that doesn’t mean I can’t enjoy the series while still recognizing its limitations.<br />
<br />
One more thing I want to highlight is the color palette. I really like it because it gives the show a distinct vibe. The colors are very strong and warm, making everything feel almost heavenly, like the characters live in some kind of romantic fantasy world completely detached from reality.<br />
<br />
I think that aesthetic reinforces the feeling of watching something idealized, a fantasy where everything looks much prettier than it would in real life. Maybe that’s also why it’s so easy to get hooked, especially when you’re specifically looking for that kind of escapism.<br />
<br />
In short, it entertains me, it has moments I enjoy, and it does a solid job of delivering what I was looking for.<br />
<br />
Is it a particularly good show? Honestly, I find it mediocre. Is it entertaining? Definitely.</span>
              </div>
