                Monster is a series with a very effective narrative structure of suspense, especially in the way it develops Johan&#039;s presence through his absence. However, its psychological and philosophical ambitions, in my opinion, overshadow the depth with which it develops its characters. Tenma seems too idealized and morally unchallenged, while Johan functions better as a mystery and narrative device than as an individual whose psychology can be understood. The series raises interesting questions about identity, goodness, responsibility, and evil, but it doesn&#039;t always develop them through conflicts that transform its characters or reveal new dimensions of them. Furthermore, some subplots, the series&#039; length, Kinderheim 511, and
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">the ending weaken my experience. Even so, I recognize its strengths in suspense, the narrative development of Johan, and several of its secondary characters. My problem isn&#039;t a lack of ideas, but rather the feeling that many of them remain unexplored to their fullest extent.</span>
              </div>
