                Well, well...<br />
Season 4 finished and now I have exactly one problem:<br />
&quot;WHAT THE HELL DO I WATCH NOW?&quot;<br />
Seriously, I started Classroom of the Elite only recently and burned through the entire thing. Season 4 just confirmed that giving this Anime a chance was one of my better decisions lately.<br />
Moving into the second school year was exactly what the series needed. Instead of simply repeating the same conflicts, we suddenly get an entire new group of first-year students entering the battlefield, and some of them are clearly VERY interested in Ayanokoji for reasons that go far beyond ordinary school competition.<br />
Tsubasa Nanase was probably one of the most
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">interesting additions for me. At first you constantly question her intentions and where exactly she stands. Watching her relationship with Ayanokoji develop throughout the season was one of my favorite parts.<br />
And of course there are several other new students who immediately feel dangerous.<br />
The White Room storyline also becomes far more important now. What previously existed mostly in Ayanokoji&#039;s past starts reaching directly into his current life, and suddenly there are people inside the school whose entire purpose revolves around him.<br />
Then you also have the third-years becoming increasingly interested in him, especially Kiryūin, plus Ryuen continuing to circle around him like two predators who recognize each other. 😄<br />
And above everything still hangs Acting Chairman Tsukishiro, who wants Ayanokoji gone.<br />
That combination makes Season 4 feel like Ayanokoji is slowly losing the luxury of remaining invisible. Too many people now understand that this apparently quiet student is definitely NOT normal.<br />
And I absolutely love that.<br />
The island examination once again proves how good Classroom of the Elite is at turning a simple school test into something resembling political and psychological warfare. Alliances, groups, betrayals, hidden motives and multiple people working against each other simultaneously.<br />
But what remains most fascinating is Ayanokoji himself.<br />
No panic. No dramatic speeches. He observes, calculates, adapts and waits until the correct moment.<br />
One thing I especially appreciate looking back at the series is how much the philosophical atmosphere of the first three seasons helped establish its identity. Those episode-opening quotations were such a small thing, but they fit Ayanokoji and the themes of the Anime perfectly.<br />
Season 4 expands the board considerably, introduces several characters I absolutely want to see more of and pushes Ayanokoji into situations where remaining in the shadows becomes increasingly difficult.<br />
Four seasons.<br />
Four Masterpieces for me.<br />
And now I am sitting here with that horrible feeling every Anime fan knows after finishing something they became completely invested in:<br />
&quot;Okay... what now?&quot; 😂<br />
I NEED MORE.<br />
WATCH IT. YOU WON&#039;T REGRET IT.</span>
              </div>
