                Well,<br />
by Season 3 I already knew what kind of monster Ayanokoji is, and somehow watching him quietly pull the strings still never gets boring.<br />
This season is less about discovering what Ayanokoji can do and more about watching just how MANY different situations he can influence at the same time without standing in the spotlight.<br />
That is one of my favorite things about Classroom of the Elite.<br />
Ayanokoji does not need everyone to recognize him as the smartest person in the room. In fact, quite the opposite. He is perfectly happy allowing somebody else to receive the credit while he works behind the scenes.<br />
Season 3 gives us more
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">class politics, alliances, betrayals and situations where students can actually lose their place at the school. The stakes increasingly feel real because intelligence alone is not enough. You need private points, information, allies and sometimes people who don&#039;t even realize they are helping you.<br />
The developments surrounding Ryuen and Honami Ichinose were especially interesting to me. Ayanokoji repeatedly interferes in situations where, from the outside, you could almost mistake his actions for simple kindness. But knowing him, you are always wondering what future purpose he already sees that everybody else has missed.<br />
That is exactly the kind of protagonist I enjoy.<br />
And then we reach the final confrontation against Class A.<br />
The chess match in particular was fantastic. It fits Ayanokoji perfectly: two intelligent people trying to read several moves ahead while the larger battle between their classes is happening around them. The fact that outside interference also becomes part of the situation only makes it more interesting.<br />
I also have to mention the philosophical quotations again. Three seasons of opening episodes with thoughts from philosophers and intellectuals gave the series a surprisingly distinctive identity. I genuinely looked forward to seeing what quote would appear and how it would relate to the episode.<br />
The supporting cast has also become much stronger by this point. Horikita, Kei, Ryuen, Ichinose and Sakayanagi are no longer simply pieces around the protagonist. They have their own ambitions and development, which makes Ayanokoji&#039;s interactions with them far more interesting.<br />
Season 3 continues exactly what I love about this series: strategy, psychology, power games and one protagonist quietly standing in the middle of everything while pretending he isn&#039;t.<br />
Ayanokoji rarely needs to raise his voice.<br />
He simply moves the pieces.<br />
Masterpiece once again.<br />
HIGHLY RECOMMENDED.</span>
              </div>
