                  <div class="thumb-right">
            <a href="https://myanimelist.net/anime/51096/Youkoso_Jitsuryoku_Shijou_Shugi_no_Kyoushitsu_e_2nd_Season" class="ga-click" data-ga-click-type="review-anime-title-pic">
              <img width="48" data-src="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1010/124180.jpg?s=401a897d07776605fccf8c7959a75c59" data-srcset="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1010/124180.jpg?s=401a897d07776605fccf8c7959a75c59 1x, https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/96x142/images/anime/1010/124180.jpg?s=63cee05639754fb038a9f650eda88b29 2x" class="lazyload" />
            </a>
          </div>
                Well,<br />
Season 2 takes everything that made the first season interesting and turns the psychological warfare up another level.<br />
And once again, Ayanokoji is the star of the show.<br />
This season really demonstrates just HOW good he is at understanding people. Not simply academically intelligent, but able to recognize fears, weaknesses and desires and then build an entire strategy around them.<br />
The development involving Kei Karuizawa was probably my favorite part of the season. Their relationship becomes much more important, and watching Ayanokoji gradually turn her into someone he can rely on for information is fascinating. The whole bullying storyline gets quite uncomfortable at times, but it also gives
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">Kei much more depth than I expected her to have.<br />
What I love about Ayanokoji is that even when he helps someone, there is always this question in the back of my mind:<br />
&quot;Is he actually helping them... or is this simply another part of his plan?&quot;<br />
That uncertainty is what makes him so entertaining.<br />
The Sports Festival was another highlight. For most of the story Ayanokoji deliberately hides what he is capable of, so whenever he decides to reveal even a fraction of his real ability it becomes an EVENT. His race against the Student Council President was one of those moments where I was basically sitting there thinking: finally, SHOW THEM. 😄<br />
But the absolute highlight comes later.<br />
Without describing the whole situation because I don&#039;t want to completely spoil it, there is a confrontation near the end where Ayanokoji finally demonstrates that his abilities are definitely not limited to strategy and academics.<br />
Holy shit.<br />
We spend so much time watching him manipulate situations quietly that seeing what happens when subtlety is no longer necessary is unbelievably satisfying. The entire sequence involving Kei, Ryuen and Ayanokoji is easily one of my favorite parts of the series so far.<br />
Again I loved the philosophical quotes opening the episodes. They fit this Anime unbelievably well because Classroom of the Elite constantly plays with ideas about power, equality, human nature, freedom and manipulation.<br />
Season 1 showed us that Ayanokoji was dangerous.<br />
Season 2 showed us WHY.<br />
Psychological games, manipulation, character development and finally one absolutely satisfying display of what Ayanokoji is really capable of.<br />
Straight-up Masterpiece.<br />
WATCH IT.</span>
              </div>
