                I have made a severe and continuous lapse in my judgement. I do not expect to be forgiven.<br />
<br />
Calling MHA the anime equivalent of ‘Chips’ was wrong. And this season proved it. <br />
<br />
Season 2 of MHA elevated the already solid 1st season to NEW heights. With solid structure, incredible fights, and FANTASTIC WRITING.<br />
<br />
This season catapulted the show to the edge of my Top 10, after only TWO SEASONS.<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">Plot:<br />
This season consisted of 3 main arcs: The Tournament, the Hero Internships, and the Battles VS the Teachers.<br />
<br />
Each arc was given the PERFECT amount of breathing room. Except for a few minor pacing issues, no scene overstayed its welcome.<br />
<br />
The fact that we had 3 arcs in one season, with REALISTIC PACING, is crazy.<br />
<br />
First, about the tournament arc. Yes, it’s a cliche. Yes, every Shonen anime needs one. But this tournament had some of the most insanely hype moments I’ve ever witnessed. Deku slamming his sled into a bunch of mines to go faster, the cavalry battles, Todoroki’s ICE MOUTAIN.<br />
<br />
The music, the buildup... Everything. It was perfect. When Deku picked Tokoyami to be on his team, I ACTUALLY cheered. In real life. I jumped off my bed and started cheering out loud. My favorite character finally got the spotlight.<br />
<br />
The tournament was DAMN-NEAR perfect. It delivered some of the best emotional payoff in Shonen, AND managed to show off the HUGE cast of characters. It didn’t leave anyone behind. In fact, the author (Kohei Horikoshi) introduced MORE characters to root for.<br />
<br />
Despite the insane 20-character main cast, Horikoshi figured out ways to give us tons of info through quick characterizations. For example, exam grades. We get to see the ranking of results, from 1st to 20th. Every character is ranked, so you understand their academic levels within… What, a minute of animation?<br />
<br />
Of course, there were still flaws. Some of the fights were based on plot armor instead of actual ability. The Bakugo VS Ochaco fight was stupid. How did she tank 50 of his punches? Taking even one of them should have blown her brains out.<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
Now, about the Internship/Hero Killer Arc. This arc was probably the weakest of the 3.<br />
<br />
The plot was decent, and the author managed to give everyone a chance to shine. He also developed the world by showing us variety in the lives of Pro Heroes.<br />
<br />
But there was one major issue: Pacing. The anime KEPT REPEATING SCENES. I probably saw the scene where Tenya declares he’ll destroy the Hero Killer 500 times.<br />
<br />
Then, the Hero Killer fight took... 4 episodes? WAY too long for one fight. Meanwhile, we only really got 2 scenes of Deku training with his new mentor.<br />
<br />
Despite the pacing issues, the actual plotting was FANTASTIC.<br />
<br />
Thank GOD the Hero Killer didn’t get away. That would have been such an unsatisfying conclusion to the arc. But again, that’s why you KILL villains when they’re restrained. You don’t LEAVE them unguarded and just let them escape. Thankfully, the author didn’t go for the bullshit trick of letting the bad guy get away.<br />
<br />
I need to praise MHA for its stellar foreshadowing. All of the ‘Random’ people that joined the fight were foreshadowed quite well. For instance, Todoroki’s Dad told him they were ‘Going somewhere’, and then he shows up to help Deku. That was actually solid writing, instead of the bullshit normal mangakas pull.<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
Finally, the Student VS Teacher Battles. Although this arc was only 5 episodes long, it’s probably some of the BEST Shonen anime has to offer.<br />
<br />
First, the fights. They were structured and executed PERFECTLY. 10 fights in 5 episodes seems insane, but they PULLED IT OFF! (Guess we didn’t need 4 episodes for the Hero Killer fight after all, huh?)<br />
<br />
The fights in this arc were some of the best Shonen fights I’ve seen. Reveals were foreshadowed, powers followed the rules, and solutions were REALISTIC. For example, Todoroki used the moment Eraser Head BLINKED to create an Ice wall. That’s STELLAR writing, because it actually follows the rules.<br />
<br />
I have to mention how HARD the magic system is. I love hard magic. Plenty of anime try to get it right, but none manage quite as well as MHA. Every character has a unique ability governed by rules. For instance, Tokoyami’s dark shadow. He summons a shadow from his back that he can use to attack or defend. Naturally, it grows stronger in darkness, and weakens when in contact with light. That’s a tiny detail, but it’s SO MUCH BETTER than just giving him an omnipotent shadow that can do whatever the fuck he wants.<br />
<br />
The attention to detail is staggering. When the Tail guy gets out of a chair, the animators DREW his tail sliding out of the chair. Another tiny detail, but it shows the thought put into the story.<br />
<br />
These final battles had some INSANE foreshadowing. When Todoroki and Momo were fighting Eraser Head, Momo lost confidence. Todoroki reminded her that the 2nd vote she received to be class president (Back in Season 1) was HIS vote, because he trusts her.<br />
<br />
WOW... Just, wow. The fact that Horikoshi managed to call back such a MINOR moment from Season 1, and turn it into character growth, is RIDICULOUS. This is some of the best writing I’ve seen, and BY FAR the best Shonen writing.<br />
<br />
Also, can we TALK about the villains? They’re FASCINATING, and they feel dangerous. Stain was a fantastic villain. He caused damage, crippled heroes, and almost killed our main cast. MHA isn’t as soft as I expected.<br />
<br />
In the final episodes of the show, we learn the backstory of Deku’s quirk and the origin of ‘All for One‘. Both reveals felt grounded, and they were given the gravity they deserved.<br />
<br />
The plot gets a 9/10 from me.<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
Animation:<br />
My Hero Academia is animated by Studio Bones, the studio behind Mob Psycho and Space Dandy.<br />
<br />
This season essentially proved that they’re my favorite studio OF ALL TIME.<br />
<br />
There were some sluggish moments. Of course. In the tournament arc we had too many stills and panning shots.<br />
<br />
But other than that, the action was PERFECT. Movements were fluid and stunning. Todoroki’s ice, for instance, always felt like a force of nature. We witnessed it fleeting across the screen and halting everything in its path. And the ice MOUNTAIN he made during the tournament gave me literal chills.<br />
<br />
I have to mention the Todoroki VS Deku fight. My fucking God. If that isn’t the BEST animation I’ve ever seen, I don’t know what is. The BLOCKS, man. The BLOCKS! It was essentially Sakuga PORN for a full 2 minutes, and it had me floored. I jumped up and actually shouted. I COULD NOT BELIEVE it was that good.<br />
<br />
The animation was fluid in general. Punches, jumps, attacks. Everything felt real. It kept me IMMERSED in the story with plenty of Sakuga.<br />
<br />
The animation gets a 9/10 from me.<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
Music:<br />
You can probably guess what I’m going to say. Yuki Hayashi, you son of a....<br />
<br />
Just when I thought it couldn’t get better, this man shocked me again. The score was SUBLIME. SUPERLATIVE. SUPREME.<br />
<br />
Hayashi leaned into the unique musical identity of MHA and made the tracks MORE special. The Rock tracks had more drum solos, and the piano tracks isolated the instrument to make scenes more emotional.<br />
<br />
The entire soundtrack shifted towards something more somber. Not only that, Hayashi created tracks that caused GENUINE fear. The Stain OSTs.<br />
<br />
But Hayashi wasn’t limited to sad tracks. The track that kicked in when the old man asked Deku ‘WHO ARE YOU?’ literally gave me chills. CHILLS. JUST FROM MUSIC.<br />
<br />
HAYASHI, YOU ARE A MASTER. 10/10 for this soundtrack.<br />
<br />
<br />
Now, about the openings and endings.<br />
<br />
The first opening, ‘Peace Sign‘ by Kenshi Yonezu, was an instant banger. Of COURSE the man behind ‘Iris Out‘ and ‘Bow and Arrow‘ was going to create a masterpiece. The visuals complemented it, showing us each character’s routines. I LOVED that little glimpse into their lives. A perfect warmup for each episode.<br />
<br />
The second opening, ‘Sora ni Utaeba‘, is… A modern classic. I have no other way to put it. It was fucking unbelievable. The powerful, slightly raspy voice of the leader singer had me hooked after the first few seconds. And then you have the beautiful angelic chorus contrasting his harsh tone (SORA NIIII UTAEBA). Fucking brilliant!<br />
<br />
The ending themes were fine. Solid music, but the visuals made them pop. I loved how we got unique characterization through the endings. We got to see the cast in a Fantasy world, which gave us more insight into their personalities.<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
Voice-Acting:<br />
All of the VAs reprised their roles and enhanced them with even more bombastic performances.<br />
<br />
Deku screamed his lungs out, Bakugo heightened his explosive nature, and Tokoyami farmed aura for the entirety of the Tournament arc.<br />
<br />
The new characters, such as Mei Hatsumi, had surprisingly interesting voices. Mei had a distinct timbre that made me want to hear her out. Shinso also had a fantastic voice. Slightly raspy, but projecting gravitas.<br />
<br />
<br />
<br />
Conclusion:<br />
This Season of MHA is, without a doubt, the BEST season of Shonen anime ever created.<br />
<br />
The structure was solid, the fights were GROUNDED, the animation was stellar, and the music almost made me bust.<br />
<br />
MHA is proving to be way more nuanced than I ever gave it credit for. It managed to develop a wide range of characters, each with depth and their own motivations.<br />
<br />
Take Tokoyami, for example. His Dark Shadow is uncontrolled emotion, and he works hard to regulate it. That’s why he’s so stoic in class.<br />
<br />
I was planning on giving this season an 8, but after writing this review, I realize that’s insufficient. Every element was perfect, including the writing.<br />
<br />
That’s why, finally, I bestow the honor of a 9/10 RATING on My Hero Academia Season 2. May all Shonen shows aspire to reach this level of perfection.<br />
<br />
The season made me feel hype I haven’t felt since Mob Psycho Season 2. Thank you, Kohei Horikoshi.</span>
              </div>
