                The Good:<br />
- The art style is amazing and unique. There&#039;s nothing cookie cutter in this show visually and it keeps things interesting.<br />
- The character development is outstanding. They really do a great job of taking you inside the minds of the individual players and give quite a bit of depth to their backgrounds as people and why they play the game.<br />
- In a way it&#039;s a typical shounen power struggle type show, but they do it in a mature way that&#039;s not cartoonish or cringey like a lot of shounen can be.<br />
- Cool opening theme<br />
- I thought the ending was well done<br />
<br />
The Bad:<br />
- The art
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">style is so unique that I found it somewhat exhausting at times as a viewer. It&#039;s hard to explain but I feel like it takes a little more effort to watch this show than it does other shows. Yes it pays off, but still it&#039;s an odd sensation.<br />
- Sometimes it feels like the art is the star of the show over the story.<br />
- As well developed as the story is it still seems like they rush through some of the background stories. That&#039;s more of a consequence of only having 11 episodes to fit everything into, but there was a lot of narrative they could have expanded on given more screen time.<br />
- Though they do it well, it&#039;s still shounen power battle anime and there&#039;s an awful lot of mediocre internal monologue and other typical shounen storylines. The actual sports narrative isn&#039;t really anything fresh or new and things can feel predictable at times.<br />
<br />
8/10, but honestly it&#039;s a 7.5/10 with an extra bump because of the cool art.</span>
