                  <div class="thumb-right">
            <a href="https://myanimelist.net/anime/59193/Mushoku_Tensei_III__Isekai_Ittara_Honki_Dasu" class="ga-click" data-ga-click-type="review-anime-title-pic">
              <img width="48" data-src="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1527/158340.jpg?s=54cf3284f484d2e65884ec4c52cefca6" data-srcset="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1527/158340.jpg?s=54cf3284f484d2e65884ec4c52cefca6 1x, https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/96x142/images/anime/1527/158340.jpg?s=d217e7b217cbece160bd3d8282b24a96 2x" class="lazyload" />
            </a>
          </div>
                I am so, so conflicted on how I feel about Jobless Reincarnation.  It consistently scores well on MAL, but I&#039;m consistently lower than most on it.  I&#039;ve had to somewhat adjust how I watch this series, and even though it has somewhat helped, it&#039;s really not what got me into the series to begin with.  I&#039;ll assume you&#039;ve seen the previous parts of Jobless Reincarnation for the purposes of this review.<br />
<br />
Story &amp; Characters: Mushoku Tensei: Jobless Reincarnation Season 3 actually takes us to Eris and we basically get to see what she&#039;s been up to since we last saw her.  It&#039;s
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">only for the first two episodes, but is is probably the best episodes of the season - for better or worse is up to you.  You can tell she has gone through some growing pains, but really has one goal - seeing Rudeus after getting much, much stronger.  She ends up going to Gull Farion, known as the Sword God.  It&#039;s there that we get to see her grow, alongside of other trainees.  Not really a spoiler, but her ability to &quot;play well&quot; with others (or lack there of) is somewhat hilarious to watch.  She&#039;s aggressive, and extremely passionate about her goal and I love it.  She&#039;s a really strong character and these two episodes reinforces that.  You see her briefly at the end of the series, and it caps off her &quot;arc&quot; in a nice way.<br />
<br />
The rest of the series revolves around Rudeus with, his now, two wives in Sylphiette and Roxy.  As individual characters, all three are strong.  They have this very firm belief that family is everything, and will do their best when they, or those close to them, need help.  And that&#039;s the biggest strength of this season: It&#039;s all about character building.  The show does such a good job at building up these characters and adding more bulk to their lives.  Rudeus has made such a strong, positive impression on those around him, that they can&#039;t help but follow him.  This includes Cliff, Zanoba and Nanahoshi.  When Rudeus and gang are off to their next quest, no matter how dangerous it might be, they all go together.<br />
<br />
That being said, what got me into Jobless Reincarnation in the first place was about the adventure and the fights within them, both of those, for the most part, are kind of absent.  Now that Rudeus has found a home, the series&#039; story mostly revolves about going from his home base and to the next destination.  Again, it&#039;s fine, but not what got me into the show to begin with.<br />
<br />
Aside the Eris arc, Nanahoshi has a pretty sizable arc.  Rudeus and gang end up traveling and meeting some pretty significant people along the way.  The conclusion in episode 10 was awesome.  The biggest problem is that there&#039;s so much weird storytelling in the middle that feels like filler that I&#039;m not sure how worth it ends up being.  Yes, it&#039;s all positive character building for minor characters, but it doesn&#039;t feel like it adds much to the macro-story of Jobless Reincarnation.<br />
<br />
Perugius is introduced during the Nanahoshi arc, and I really was expecting him to be an old geezer with not much to say.  I was very, very wrong.  Boring as her may come off as being, he listens well and has some great things to say.  I was surprised that I liked him as much as I did.<br />
<br />
What does add is the flashback in episode 13.  This is flashbacks done right.  It&#039;s aggressive, violent and really quite shocking.  I saw online that they actually did hold back based on what the original source material said in terms of the levels of violence that was initially shown, which is somewhat disappointing.  That being said, we get the idea of what they were going for, and more.  But this somewhat reinforces my point - this season as very obvious highpoints, but with too many low points in between.  (Story: 6/10, Characters: 8/10)<br />
<br />
Art: Studio Bind has gone a pretty good job and giving this series a steady level of animation.  Previous seasons had bumps along the way, but based on what I saw, it looked a little more polished this time around.  (8/10)<br />
<br />
Sound: I really like this series&#039; approach to their opening theme, as it&#039;s a not always a pre-determined bunch of scenes, but rather, shows us part of the story instead.  It&#039;s got obvious cons, but is a net positive in my eyes.  Voice work remains strong here.  I love hearing Lindsay Seidel (Eris) back voicing, as I really haven&#039;t heard her in much these days.  This is probably Suzie Yeung (Nanahoshi) at her best, though I&#039;m really not surprised at this point.  Bradley Gareth (Gull) and Kenny James (Perugius) apparently voiced in previous parts, but I don&#039;t recall them saying much - they were great here.  (9/10)<br />
<br />
Overall: Mushoku Tensei: Jobless Reincarnation Season 3 had really solid high points, but I&#039;ve obviously expressed my disappointment in others.  I&#039;m 100% with you if you like this series because it tries to do its best to be right by its characters.  I get that.  And if you were to tell me that the series isn&#039;t about worldbuilding and action, then I&#039;d obviously believe you.  I plan to watch it right until the end regardless, but if you *really* want to watch it for the season reasons I did, you might be lower on it than I am.  (8/10)</span>
              </div>
