          </div>
                Demon Slayer: Kimetsu no Yaiba Infinity Castle I is the first part of the three-part Demon Slayer movie coming.  And finally, after a year, it was released on Crunchyroll.  I&#039;ll assume you&#039;ve seen the previous parts for the purposes of this review.<br />
<br />
Story &amp; Characters: I don&#039;t think there&#039;s any secret of what Demon Slayer&#039;s about at this point.  Tanjiro is on his quest to both find a way to return his sister, Nezuko, into a human, and to slay the head of all demons, Muzan.  Now, Tanjiro and company enter his Infinity Castle, where he, all the Hashira and several other
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">members of the Demon Slayer Corps. are stuck, duking it out with any demon that heads their way.  The three-part movie kind of comes down as a necessity, due to all the concurrent fights going on at the same time.  We&#039;ll tackle them one by one here.<br />
<br />
Shinobu vs Upper Moon Two, Douma: This is a decently solid fight.  We really haven&#039;t seen Shinobu tasked with fighting a difficult foe yet, unlike many of the other Hashira, so seeing how she fights does feel pretty refreshing.  She fights differently than many other Demon Slayers, and you can tell her intelligence gives her the edge on most any foe.  Kanao makes an appearance late in the fight, and even though she doesn&#039;t really add much herself, her connection to Shinbou remains strong.  As per usual, we get to see some of the past of Douma, and even though the flashbacks are usually pretty intrusive to the pacing, I actually thought they kept it brief here.<br />
<br />
Zenitsu vs Upper Moon Six, Kaigaku: Kaigaku replaced Daki and Gyuutarou after the Entertainment District as Upper Moon Six, which we saw briefly after the conclusion of one of the seasons.  Though we don&#039;t know much about him, his connection to Zenitsu becomes much more clearer, and I was also a fan of how this one panned out.  Like with Douma, they kept the flashback brief.  This is a very different story from the one prior and it remains flashy to watch... for obvious reasons.  This kind of resolves how I feel about Zenitsu as a character, even if he clearly is the &quot;third&quot; among the intimal trio.<br />
<br />
Tanjiro and Giyuu vs Upper Moon Three, Akaza: Getting to see Tanjiro and Giyuu fight together is awesome to experience after all this time.  They made a conscience effort to remind us that Tanjiro is very clearly not at Giyuu&#039;s level in terms of fighting skill, and I like that.  Even though this is probably the best of the three fights, they really overcorrected with how much they think we would like Akaza&#039;s story.  Don&#039;t get me wrong, his past is *the* best out of any of the Upper or Lower Moon demons so far, but the pacing here is easily the worst.  We continually get stuck in a loop during the current time and while I get a lot of it is about his conflicted past, they overdid it.  The two main characters, along with the story, were really strong, but this could have been executed a little better.<br />
<br />
Upon the conclusion of these three fights, our gang continues onto the castle, and, my assumption, is we&#039;ll see more of the Hashira in action.  We obviously have a lot more demons to go, and I can&#039;t wait to see it - whenever it airs.  (Story: 7/10, Characters: 9/10)<br />
<br />
Art: The art and animation remain some of ufotable&#039;s top work - and that&#039;s a big compliment, coming from the same studio that produced other masterpieces like those in the Fate series.  (10/10)<br />
<br />
Sound: The work of Yuki Kajiura is obvious, and her all in this series should be highly commended.  I&#039;ve said it before, but Demon Slayer has attracted some of the top VO talent on earth, and the fact that every single Hashira is voiced by such a big name speaks volumes.  Channing Tatum joins the movie in a minor role - apparently his daughter&#039;s a big fan of the series, which is awesome.  Rebecca Wang also joins in a minor role, as is very strong here.  I&#039;m a little surprised Kyle McCarley (Yushiro) and Laura Post (Tamayo) returned, as both have said their pieces against various works in the past.  I wouldn&#039;t be shocked if this was a union works, but perhaps it is, due to what the movie is.  (10/10)<br />
<br />
Overall: Demon Slayer: Kimetsu no Yaiba Infinity Castle I is the first of three parts of Demon Slayer movies and there&#039;s no doubt that it starts out very strong.  Top notch story telling remains a strong point, but the series just can&#039;t get away from its bad pacing.  This isn&#039;t to say it ruins the movie, but when the same criticism remains in every Demon Slayer part, you kind of expect it.  Still, the series is extremely strong, and I wouldn&#039;t dare miss the rest.  (9/10)</span>
