                  <div class="thumb-right">
            <a href="https://myanimelist.net/anime/37994/B__The_Beginning_Succession" class="ga-click" data-ga-click-type="review-anime-title-pic">
              <img width="48" data-src="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1409/112177.jpg?s=37ef57174166c426f67c7d6dfda0da7a" data-srcset="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1409/112177.jpg?s=37ef57174166c426f67c7d6dfda0da7a 1x, https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/96x142/images/anime/1409/112177.jpg?s=ffd7e69bd9c328dfeadf60985b93f974 2x" class="lazyload" />
            </a>
          </div>
                I have never seen an anime so blatantly tell me to go fuck myself and that my time was wasted as I have with B the Beginning season 2.<br />
<br />
I loved B the Beginning season 1 and it is one of my favorites. B the Beginning Season 2 starts on a strong note for the first half of the first episode. Then they decided to regress Keith Flick as a character.<br />
<br />
Keith&#039;s journey in season 1 is learning to trust others and chasing a revenge that he ultimately forsakes in order to save the people that he loves. They tell him that he doesn&#039;t need to fight
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">alone anymore. So season 2 starts with him walking into a trap, alone, and getting turned into a plot device. I think Episode 4, talking about how Keith fell in love with his step-sister was disgusting. I think that making that a plot point really took away from his character. His conversation with Erika and Gilbert weren&#039;t satisfying either. That&#039;s to say nothing of what the show does to Lily.<br />
<br />
Lily was supposed to be just as capable and smart as Keith. So naturally, she spends the whole series playing Initial D and contributing nothing to the plot. But it gets better when you realize she was supposed to do something to help find Keith. But it doesn&#039;t matter, because the show can&#039;t even commit to its own plot or the story it wants to tell.<br />
<br />
The group needs to search 4 towers to find Keith. They split up, with 4 people moving to cover 3 towers. Lily doesn&#039;t do anything. Yuna being treated like she needs to be carried is an insult to what we saw from her in season 1 too. I was okay with Izanami being back but that also amounts to pretty much nothing.<br />
<br />
The show isn&#039;t confident to commit to anything. The series starts with Koku being held hostage with Keith being captured, and being threatened to comply or that Lily and Yuna would be next. Koku, instead, rebels against Kirisame and instead of killing Keith or following through on their threat, they do nothing. Then, suddenly, instead of needing Koku they can copy his abilities to a new Gen Jetblack or whatever the fuck they wanted to call that blonde girl. But then they don&#039;t even do that because Koku beats her in a poorly animated slow motion slog of a fight.<br />
<br />
The King and his successor are irrelevant, but apparently Kirisame was playing a double agent. For why? Fuck if I know. We learned nothing about Kirisame. He showed up four times, two of which were just the bad guys (?) saying &quot;it&#039;s just like you planned.&quot; What plan? Excuse me? We don&#039;t know his motives. At the start we are told that he wants to &quot;rid the world of the reggies so that they can start a new world.&quot; By the end, we learn that this isn&#039;t true, because why else would he betray the successor and work for the king? Who is his posse and why do I care? And instead of explaining, the show ends on a fight between Kirisame and Koku where Kirisame says &quot;you are ignorant of everything.&quot; Okay. Enlighten me. What am I missing?<br />
<br />
This whole season feels like the outer frame of a puzzle with every interior piece missing. Outside of Keith Flick&#039;s character assassination, Lily&#039;s uselessness, or the random bs with Asagiri, I do believe that there was a framework here for an amazing followup to B the Beginning. Unfortunately, compressing it into 6 episodes and a complete and utter lack of direction or endgame leaves the entire season wasting every episode trying to find some new direction to propel itself before finally ending just when we might be getting started.<br />
<br />
It&#039;s such a disappointment when the original series, while convoluted, was perfect and upon a rewatch can be enjoyed even better. This feels like how in the 90s they&#039;d release amazing OVAs, only to follow them up with a full season, direct-to-VHS anime series that would completely miss the point of the original, except in reverse. I can&#039;t say I just want it to end here though. I feel like Production I.G. owes me a third season for wasting not only the time I spent on this season, but the time I spent waiting for it to come out, and the years I spent avoiding it because I was scared it wouldn&#039;t hold up to the original. Production I.G. needs to renew B the Beginning and try again with a full length season. Not some tie-in movie that shortens the original manga arc and misses the point.</span>
              </div>
