          </div><div class="tags"><div class="tag mixed-feelings btn-label js-btn-label" data-id="2" data-href="https://myanimelist.net/anime/61169/Black_Torch/reviews"><i class="fas fa-star-half-stroke"></i>Mixed Feelings</div><div class="tag spoiler">Spoiler</div></div>      <div class="text">
                  <div class="thumb-right">
            <a href="https://myanimelist.net/anime/61169/Black_Torch" class="ga-click" data-ga-click-type="review-anime-title-pic">
              <img width="48" data-src="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1965/158363.jpg?s=598f488c48a183c587a2fbd334233cfa" data-srcset="https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/48x71/images/anime/1965/158363.jpg?s=598f488c48a183c587a2fbd334233cfa 1x, https://cdn.myanimelist.net/r/96x142/images/anime/1965/158363.jpg?s=04db3c4ff7e6ffb10e6b805a6a4ac22d 2x" class="lazyload" />
            </a>
          </div>
                ⚠ SPOILERS AHEAD ⚠<br />
<br />
There is a lot of potential in Black Torch. And honestly, that&#039;s exactly what disappoints me the most about it.<br />
<br />
The Mononoke and their amazing character designs, paired with the premise, make this feel like it has all the makings of a really good action anime. The problem is that, after finishing the show, I feel like I&#039;ve seen far more of what it could be than what it actually is ( ￣︿￣)<br />
<br />
And that brings me to my biggest issue with this series: THE COMBAT... or rather the lack of it!<br />
<br />
It&#039;s not even that I disliked one particular fight or that there were
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">a few fights that didn&#039;t live up to the hype. It&#039;s that, time and time again, the show sets up a confrontation, makes it look like something exciting is about to happen, and then fails to deliver ANYTHING!<br />
<br />
I don&#039;t need every single fight to be some masterpiece of animation. But I do need the anime to actually SHOW ME THE FIGHT! If a character is going to unleash an attack, then let me see the attack. If two characters are going to clash, then let me experience that clash instead of having the most interesting parts barely shown IF AT ALL.<br />
<br />
And the opening theme makes this even more frustrating.<br />
<br />
There are some genuinely cool visuals in that opening that made me wish the actual anime had done more with its characters. Take Koga, for example. There&#039;s a scene where he&#039;s walking toward the camera, his horn begins to manifest, and the horn pushes his hood back as he continues walking. It&#039;s such a simple thing, but it looks incredibly cool. It gives him a presence that makes you want to see more of him.<br />
<br />
Except we don&#039;t get that scene in the actual show.<br />
<br />
Then there&#039;s the part near the beginning where Jiro&#039;s frame overlaps with Rago&#039;s. Rago&#039;s eyes light up, and we see that shadowy, distorted version of his true form emerge. THAT is the kind of design I wanted to see!<br />
<br />
And then we get to Rago&#039;s actual true form in the anime, which looks like a solid 3D-rendered object that someone dropped into the show!<br />
<br />
I&#039;m not even exaggerating when I say that it looks like someone came up with a rough concept for Rago&#039;s true form, rendered it in 3D, and then kept it in as the final design.<br />
<br />
What&#039;s even more confusing is that the show itself gives us a reason to question this decision.<br />
<br />
In episode 11, we see these giant flying bugs, and they look considerably better to me. Their teeth look better, their animation looks better, and they generally feel more convincing as giant creatures. So why does Rago&#039;s true form look so much worse?<br />
<br />
We&#039;ve had anime like Naruto animating enormous creatures for decades. This isn&#039;t some impossible challenge that nobody has figured out how to handle. And I could understand using CGI if Rago&#039;s true form were constantly moving around, fighting dynamically, and performing all kinds of complicated attacks. At least then, I&#039;d understand what the production was trying to accomplish.<br />
<br />
But what does Rago actually do?<br />
<br />
He hops around a few times... that&#039;s it! why did they have to render that in 3D? LOL<br />
<br />
That&#039;s pretty much what we&#039;ve seen from him in terms of action so far. And for that, we get a design that looks so noticeably out of place!!<br />
<br />
I just don&#039;t get it.<br />
<br />
And after all of this, I was still hoping that the final confrontation would finally give me something worth getting excited about.<br />
<br />
At the end of episode 11, Jiro and Amagi&#039;s clash begins, and it looks promising... then I remembered that there was only one episode left.<br />
<br />
Even if the entirety of episode 12 were dedicated to their fight, which it wasn&#039;t... would that really be enough time to deliver the kind of epic clash this setup seemed to promise?!<br />
<br />
NO! the fight was okay.<br />
<br />
That&#039;s it. It was okay.<br />
<br />
To give it credit, it&#039;s definitely the best fight in Black Torch. Compared to the other fights in this anime, it actually feels epic. But that&#039;s also a pretty low bar to clear when ALL of the previous fights have been disappointing. Put it next to the fights in other action anime, and I don&#039;t think it would be particularly impressive.<br />
<br />
And that&#039;s the problem. Even when the show finally gives us its biggest confrontation, the one that should leave us with a strong impression of what this series can do, I still come away thinking about how much better it could have been.<br />
<br />
However, there is one thing I genuinely liked about the ending, and that&#039;s Amagi himself.<br />
<br />
When Jiro punches a hole through his chest, there&#039;s a subtle shift in Amagi&#039;s facial expression as he realizes that it&#039;s over for him. What I liked about this moment is that, despite everything, he stays true to his convictions until the very end.<br />
<br />
Just moments earlier, Amagi had said he would destroy Jiro if he couldn&#039;t obtain Rago&#039;s power. Yet, even then, it never felt like he acted out of any personal hatred toward Jiro. Jiro was simply standing in the way of his goal, and Amagi was willing to eliminate him to achieve it. But when the moment finally comes and Amagi realizes he&#039;s lost, he accepts that his goal is no longer attainable rather than becoming petty and trying to take Jiro down with him out of spite. With Jiro completely defenseless in front of him, Amagi could have still tried to kill him and take him down with him, yet he chooses not to.<br />
<br />
And that&#039;s what I liked most about his ending.<br />
<br />
It&#039;s a shame that the fight itself couldn&#039;t leave me with a cool impression!<br />
<br />
At the end of the episode we get the closing line from Jiro: &quot;The mission&#039;s a go.&quot;<br />
<br />
It definitely is a tease for a continuation! If we do get more of Black Torch, I&#039;d genuinely like to see what it could become with a better execution of its action!!<br />
<br />
Because I can see a GREAT DEAL of potential here.<br />
<br />
I really can!<br />
<br />
But potential only gets you so far. At some point, the actual show has to deliver on it, and that&#039;s where Black Torch disappointed me. Black Torch is an action-packed anime. The action is supposed to be one of its biggest selling points! And yet, somehow, there was a lot of action in it. They just weren&#039;t actioning LOL.<br />
<br />
I know that sounds ridiculous, but I genuinely don&#039;t know how else to put it.<br />
<br />
Let&#039;s just say that I wanted to come away from this series remembering the fights and the moments where the characters finally went all out.<br />
<br />
Instead, I&#039;m left thinking about all the action I wish the anime had actually shown me ༼ ◕ω◕ ༽</span>
              </div>
