What makes this show great is how well it handles the military and political side of things. The character dynamics are solid, and Aoi Yuki’s voice acting for Tanya is phenomenal as always. The animation during the aerial combat scenes is also just fascinating to watch, and I really enjoyed the conflicting motivations of characters like Zettour and Mary Sue.<br />
<br />
The season definitely slowed down a bit in the middle, focusing heavily on political dialogue—whether to surrender, push forward with the war, or agree to peace talks. While
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">I actually didn&#039;t mind the political depth, my biggest complaint this season is that the author feels a bit too hesitant to kill off named characters. Sure, Rudersdorf died, but that happened right at the very end. All of Tanya&#039;s core subordinates are still breathing, and major antagonists like Mary Sue are still around after all this time. Plus, I seriously doubt that Mikel guy is actually gone for good off-screen—he&#039;s definitely going to pop back up later.<br />
<br />
Overall, I still really like the visuals, the core premise, and where the story is heading. I just hope future seasons bring back some real consequences and higher stakes to the war, especially since it&#039;s supposed to be a dark fantasy.</span>
              </div>
