                What initially seemed like a fresh and unique animation style quickly revealed a much bigger problem: pacing. Despite being marketed as a standard 20-minute anime, a surprising amount of each episode is filled with content that adds little to the actual story progression.<br />
<br />
The recap and opening alone consume nearly five minutes, while the ending takes another two. That leaves roughly 13 minutes of episode content, but even that is stretched further with repeated animation sequences, recycled scenes, and frequent flashbacks. As a result, the amount of genuinely new material often feels closer to 10 minutes, sometimes even less.<br />
<br />
The excessive padding becomes increasingly noticeable over time.
                  <span class="js-visible" style="margin-left: -4px;">...</span>
          <span class="js-hidden" style="display: none;">Rather than maintaining momentum, episodes regularly revisit previously shown content or reuse the same animation cuts, making the experience feel repetitive and artificially prolonged.<br />
<br />
The story itself could likely be told far more effectively in 12 episodes. Instead, it feels as though enough unnecessary filler has been added to stretch the series to 26 episodes, resulting in slower pacing and a less engaging viewing experience overall.</span>
              </div>
